2025-05-01 03:13:44 - Thread-1229 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• player.player_id : unique id to join with every other table  
• player.player_name : name that has to be returned in the final answer  
• ball_by_ball.match_id : used to join every ball with its match  
• ball_by_ball.striker : the batsman (‑> player_id) who faced the ball  
• batsman_scored.runs_scored : runs got off that very delivery – will be summed per player per match  
• batsman_scored.(match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no) + the same 4 columns in ball_by_ball → composite key to join runs to the striker  
• player_match.(match_id, player_id, team_id) : tells which team a given player represented in that match  
• match.match_id : join field between player_match / ball_by_ball and match result  
• match.match_winner : id of the winning team – lets us know who lost  

Main conditions we will finally need  

1. SUM(runs_scored) ≥ 100  (grouped by match_id, player_id)  
2. player_match.team_id ≠ match.match_winner (the player’s team lost)  
3. match.match_winner IS NOT NULL (only decided games)

Below are ten exploration‑to‑solution queries (each LIMITED to 20 rows) that gradually build up to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at the runs‐per‐ball ledger
SELECT *
FROM "batsman_scored"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Join runs with the striker so we see who scored every ball
SELECT b.match_id, b.over_id, b.ball_id, b.innings_no,
       b.striker AS player_id, s.runs_scored
FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
  ON   b.match_id = s.match_id
 AND   b.over_id  = s.over_id
 AND   b.ball_id  = s.ball_id
 AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Aggregate: total runs by each batsman in each match
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.striker            AS player_id,
       SUM(s.runs_scored)   AS total_runs
FROM   "ball_by_ball"   AS b
JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
  ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
 AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
 AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
 AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Keep only player–match pairs where 100+ runs were scored
SELECT *
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball"   AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
) AS x
WHERE  x.total_runs >= 100
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Add player names to those 100+ efforts
SELECT p.player_name,
       x.match_id,
       x.total_runs
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id = x.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Attach the team each centurion represented in that match
SELECT p.player_name, x.match_id,
       pm.team_id, x.total_runs
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player_match" AS pm
  ON pm.match_id  = x.match_id
 AND pm.player_id = x.player_id
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id  = x.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Bring in match result information
SELECT p.player_name, x.match_id,
       pm.team_id, m.match_winner,
       x.total_runs
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player_match" AS pm
  ON pm.match_id  = x.match_id
 AND pm.player_id = x.player_id
JOIN "match" AS m
  ON m.match_id = x.match_id
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id = x.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Keep rows where the batsman's team actually lost
SELECT p.player_name, x.match_id,
       pm.team_id, m.match_winner,
       x.total_runs
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball" AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player_match" AS pm
  ON pm.match_id  = x.match_id
 AND pm.player_id = x.player_id
JOIN "match" AS m
  ON m.match_id = x.match_id
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id = x.player_id
WHERE m.match_winner IS NOT NULL
  AND pm.team_id <> m.match_winner
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Distinct list of such players (no duplicate names)
SELECT DISTINCT p.player_name
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball"   AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player_match" AS pm
  ON pm.match_id  = x.match_id
 AND pm.player_id = x.player_id
JOIN "match" AS m
  ON m.match_id = x.match_id
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id = x.player_id
WHERE m.match_winner IS NOT NULL
  AND pm.team_id <> m.match_winner
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL answer (ordered alphabetically, still limited to 20 for inspection)
SELECT DISTINCT p.player_name AS "Player Name"
FROM (
      SELECT b.match_id,
             b.striker          AS player_id,
             SUM(s.runs_scored) AS total_runs
      FROM   "ball_by_ball"   AS b
      JOIN   "batsman_scored" AS s
        ON   b.match_id   = s.match_id
       AND   b.over_id    = s.over_id
       AND   b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
       AND   b.innings_no = s.innings_no
      GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
      HAVING SUM(s.runs_scored) >= 100
) AS x
JOIN "player_match" AS pm
  ON pm.match_id  = x.match_id
 AND pm.player_id = x.player_id
JOIN "match" AS m
  ON m.match_id = x.match_id
JOIN "player" AS p
  ON p.player_id = x.player_id
WHERE m.match_winner IS NOT NULL
  AND pm.team_id <> m.match_winner
ORDER BY p.player_name
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries move step‑by‑step from basic inspection to the complete solution, fulfilling the requirement to find players who hit centuries but still ended up on the losing side.
